b/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667932} {:answer/id #uuid "478db39d-a240-5926-ab48-11c8e190272d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8899c4e3-2645-5f8b-9127-fbf8c6f48ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667932} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae205eab-db1e-56c2-ac7d-5bc619d0d0ac" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d9bc49-c94e-5174-9068-2abf75c42684" :question/math-academy-id "q-128490" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true."}