b/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bbc454b-136b-559e-ae09-8d1685b0819e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48346" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff6f5e2fbd530ee9d90c2adf02bb212886fb137c1d55c9e9d78958902ccc9f13.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,1].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[1,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(1,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-1,1).$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and this contains the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [1,\\infty )$; $x\\in (-1,1)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397cf0e7-531b-59b4-993a-81a61e57be0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459425} {:answer/id #uuid "e21d801d-2a49-5b97-b536-69926270c5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397cf0e7-531b-59b4-993a-81a61e57be0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459425} :answer-f