#edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439793} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb8b233-4412-59f3-a92a-f4dd74c47a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31c8d9af-b5d1-5e36-a751-1fe872b23795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439793} :answer-field/id #uuid "306b7a0f-9d47-5fe6-b0da-2a37dbd3359d" :answer-field/key "$x=9$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c727f164-6d45-54e1-9dc0-d63951b5c8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668084} {:answer/id #uuid "ef82de42-904e-5b60-b8b1-e523fa7c812b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c727f164-6d45-54e1-9dc0-d63951b5c8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668084} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f365be-7ae2-5b1c-8d4b-570b0cea6dfb" :answer-field/key "No vertical asymptotes" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683930f5-7231-57ef-90a9-236b0f94178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4694" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-27} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}-{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the difference of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-3)({x}^{2}+3x+9)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $(x-3)=0$ gives the solution $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3x+9=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(9) \\\\  & =9-36 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7e8e9d-f212-5d13-a5be-734b534e5ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439795} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea5db37-0c6c-57f3-aa92-22d599e01482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7e8e9d-f212-5d13-a5be-734b534e5ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439795} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a10d88-9b25-5ba8-a920-18c9befa1b6e" :answer-field/key "$x=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440255} {:answer-field/choic